#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074538} :activity/id #uuid "b07ed8c4-8ba9-5778-a813-94fb9ccc2843" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050295 :topic/id #uuid "3d7bd7bf-6970-5ce8-8de1-27d53b866227" :topic/math-academy-id 510} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074992 :tutorial/content "Consider the right triangle below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd056cb2c92d918412778089c0744525fb676a79047b0e779161c68db8210d54.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin A = \\frac{BC}{AC}, & \\cos C = \\frac{BC}{AC}, \\\\ \\cos A = \\frac{AB}{AC}, & \\sin C = \\frac{AB}{AC}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\sin A = \\cos C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\sin C\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nm\\angle A =\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle C$ are complementary, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = 90^\\circ - \\theta\n$$\n\nand we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\cos (90^{∘} - θ) \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n- the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement, and\n- the cosine of an angle equals the sine of its complement.\n\nSine and cosine are known as **cofunctions**. The latter is called **co**sine to indicate that it is the **co**function of sine." :tutorial/id #uuid "3b432c9c-d757-5975-9b24-999b2bce5a31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2456 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c97a3887-eade-5923-b47f-6b98202badf5" :step/math-academy-id 10997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073616 :question/id #uuid "83830700-8d9b-5205-8f3e-764247162987" :question/math-academy-id "e-3266"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b7cb13a-a561-5a95-a87c-5d2f2a59b08e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074026}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Trigonometric Ratio as a Cofunction of a Complementary Angle"} :step/id #uuid "5b219563-3ca8-59a2-ad6d-59b8d4876029" :step/math-academy-id 10998 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073617 :question/id #uuid "320908eb-adaf-5516-9810-a4fae4dc8f38" :question/math-academy-id "e-3268"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f46344-b7c0-51a1-918c-aaac34dd5c40" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074254}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Satisfying a Cofunction Equality"} :step/id #uuid "ea4cf402-26be-57c6-a0f8-f6d5d0730154" :step/math-academy-id 10999 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074541}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073618 :question/id #uuid "27b53e09-978d-5d78-aa3f-ad9c780adfeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3270"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1ec0eb3-ea8e-5783-b6a2-dc7128709f71" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049496}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233293}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Trigonometric Ratio With a Linear Expression Angle as a Cofunction of a Complementary Angle"} :step/id #uuid "5c679c15-81dd-565b-89b0-c0e2d65c49af" :step/math-academy-id 11001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073619 :question/id #uuid "bd79dabb-70aa-58d8-9273-f15fed5d65cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3271"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b653137f-4991-5388-b34d-8573c58c0841" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074106}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given Two Angles and a Cofunction Equality"} :step/id #uuid "7d64db21-880b-5b14-9446-eb999ee3cd8e" :step/math-academy-id 11002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075000 :tutorial/content "So far, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\cos (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the above properties, we can also derive cofunction properties for secant and cosecant. We start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec θ &= \\frac{1}{\\cos θ} \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\sin (90^{∘} - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\csc (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the same reasoning, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc θ &= \\sec (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have deduced that secant and **co**secant are also cofunctions. To summarize:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec θ &= \\csc (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\csc θ &= \\sec (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by similar reasoning, it's possible to show that tangent and **co**tangent are cofunctions. Their cofunction relationships are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\cot (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\cot θ &= \\tan (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04166248-ef12-5aa7-b46b-1a077920d60c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2562 :tutorial/title "Reciprocal Functions of Complementary Angles"} :step/id #uuid "60e93782-7e78-571a-affc-f9bf9070f1e5" :step/math-academy-id 11755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074539}}] :activity/title "Trigonometric Functions of Complementary Angles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073937}]}
